1
00:00:56,530 --> 00:00:59,530
Good morning.

2
00:00:59,530 --> 00:01:01,530
What a wonderful morning!

3
00:01:01,530 --> 00:01:03,530
Don't the flowers smell wonderful?

4
00:01:03,530 --> 00:01:05,530
Good morning, Ellen.

5
00:01:05,530 --> 00:01:07,530
Yes, they do.

6
00:01:07,530 --> 00:01:10,530
That's why I'm reading my paper and having my coffee

7
00:01:10,530 --> 00:01:12,530
on the patio this morning.

8
00:01:12,530 --> 00:01:17,530
Ah, it does smell sweet.

9
00:01:17,530 --> 00:01:20,530
How was your school-board meeting last night?

10
00:01:20,530 --> 00:01:22,530
You must've come home very late.

11
00:01:22,530 --> 00:01:24,530
Did you find the sandwich I made for you?

12
00:01:24,530 --> 00:01:26,530
Thanks, dear.

13
00:01:26,530 --> 00:01:29,530
I was so tired

14
00:01:29,530 --> 00:01:30,530
I didn't even finish it.

15
00:01:30,530 --> 00:01:37,530
Philip, I've been working on this special project Philip

16
00:01:37,530 --> 00:01:38,530
with the school board,

17
00:01:38,530 --> 00:01:40,530
and I'd like your opinion about it.

18
00:01:40,530 --> 00:01:42,530
What is it?

19
00:01:42,530 --> 00:01:45,530
I've been trying to find

20
00:01:45,530 --> 00:01:47,530
a way to encourage reading.

21
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:50,530
Good luck!

22
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:53,530
Well, I think I may have found a way to do it.

23
00:01:53,530 --> 00:01:54,530
Tell me about it.

24
00:01:54,530 --> 00:01:57,530
I work with families every day, Ellen.

25
00:01:57,530 --> 00:01:59,530
I see how people spend their leisure time--

26
00:01:59,530 --> 00:02:01,530
young and old.

27
00:02:01,530 --> 00:02:03,530
Mostly watching television.

28
00:02:03,530 --> 00:02:05,530
Well, that would be OK

29
00:02:05,530 --> 00:02:07,530
if, and I repeat,

30
00:02:07,530 --> 00:02:09,530
if people took the time to read.

31
00:02:09,530 --> 00:02:11,530
I couldn't agree with you more.

32
00:02:11,530 --> 00:02:13,530
The question is,

33
00:02:13,530 --> 00:02:16,530
how do we get them to read more?

34
00:02:16,530 --> 00:02:18,530
I think

35
00:02:18,530 --> 00:02:22,530
you're going to give me the answer to that question.

36
00:02:22,530 --> 00:02:24,530
You have that look in your eye.

37
00:02:24,530 --> 00:02:26,530
I do have an answer, Philip.

38
00:02:26,530 --> 00:02:27,530
Or at least I think I do.

39
00:02:27,530 --> 00:02:31,530
Well, tell me about it.

40
00:02:31,530 --> 00:02:33,530
The plan is a simple one.

41
00:02:33,530 --> 00:02:38,530
Involve the entire family in a reading project.

42
00:02:38,530 --> 00:02:40,530
In the home?

43
00:02:40,530 --> 00:02:42,530
Yes, in the home.

44
00:02:42,530 --> 00:02:43,530
But first in the school-rooms.

45
00:02:43,530 --> 00:02:46,530
Hmm, interesting.

46
00:02:46,530 --> 00:02:50,530
But how do you plan to do that?

47
00:02:50,530 --> 00:02:53,530
By arranging with the public schools

48
00:02:53,530 --> 00:02:56,530
to schedule one hour a week--to start with.

49
00:02:56,530 --> 00:02:58,530
During that time

50
00:02:58,530 --> 00:03:00,530
parents are invited to attend--

51
00:03:00,530 --> 00:03:02,530
and to read along with the children--their children.

52
00:03:02,530 --> 00:03:07,530
It can go beyond the school system, Ellen.

53
00:03:07,530 --> 00:03:09,530
Really?

54
00:03:09,530 --> 00:03:15,530
I guarantee you it would go very well in the hospitals.

55
00:03:15,530 --> 00:03:17,530
My patients--mostly kids--

56
00:03:17,530 --> 00:03:20,530
would love to read and be read to.

57
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:22,530
You think so?

58
00:03:22,530 --> 00:03:24,530
I know so.

59
00:03:24,530 --> 00:03:26,530
Maybe we can experiment with your patients

60
00:03:26,530 --> 00:03:29,530
and see how the plan works.

61
00:03:29,530 --> 00:03:31,530
I love the idea.

62
00:03:31,530 --> 00:03:37,530
Would you work with me on it?

63
00:03:37,530 --> 00:03:41,530
I would love to, Philip.

64
00:03:41,530 --> 00:03:45,530
And that way,

65
00:03:45,530 --> 00:03:48,530
we'll spend more time together, Ellen.

66
00:03:48,530 --> 00:03:52,530
We just don't see each other anymore.

67
00:03:52,530 --> 00:03:55,530
You and I are very busy these days.

68
00:03:55,530 --> 00:03:56,530
This is true.

69
00:03:56,530 --> 00:03:59,530
We need to find time to be together more,

70
00:03:59,530 --> 00:04:01,530
to do things together more--you and I.

71
00:04:01,530 --> 00:04:04,530
This would be a wonderful way to accomplish that.

72
00:04:04,530 --> 00:04:05,530
I have a question.

73
00:04:05,530 --> 00:04:06,530
Yes?

74
00:04:06,530 --> 00:04:09,530
What do we read?

75
00:04:09,530 --> 00:04:11,530
To the patients in the ward?

76
00:04:11,530 --> 00:04:12,530
Yes.

77
00:04:12,530 --> 00:04:14,530
Well,

78
00:04:14,530 --> 00:04:16,530
let you and I talk about it.

79
00:04:16,530 --> 00:04:30,530
What would you like to read to them?

80
00:04:30,530 --> 00:04:33,530
Mrs. Stewart and I Stewart

81
00:04:33,530 --> 00:04:35,530
will read a poem by Robert Frost.

82
00:04:35,530 --> 00:04:37,530
It's called

83
00:04:37,530 --> 00:04:39,530
"Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening."

84
00:04:39,530 --> 00:04:42,530
Would you begin, Ellen?

85
00:04:42,530 --> 00:04:43,530
All right.

86
00:04:43,530 --> 00:04:48,530
"Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening"

87
00:04:48,530 --> 00:04:51,530
by Robert Frost. Robert Frost

88
00:04:51,530 --> 00:04:55,530
Whose woods these are I think I know.

89
00:04:55,530 --> 00:04:57,530
His house is in the village though;

90
00:04:57,530 --> 00:05:00,530
He will not see me stopping here

91
00:05:00,530 --> 00:05:03,530
To watch his woods fill up with snow.

92
00:05:03,530 --> 00:05:06,530
My little horse must think it queer

93
00:05:06,530 --> 00:05:08,530
To stop without a farmhouse near

94
00:05:08,530 --> 00:05:11,530
Between the woods and frozen lake

95
00:05:11,530 --> 00:05:15,530
The darkest evening of the year.

96
00:05:15,530 --> 00:05:18,530
He gives his harness bells a shake

97
00:05:18,530 --> 00:05:20,530
To ask if there is some mistake.

98
00:05:20,530 --> 00:05:23,530
The only other sound's the sweep

99
00:05:23,530 --> 00:05:26,530
Of easy wind and downy flake.

100
00:05:26,530 --> 00:05:30,530
The woods are lovely, dark and deep,

101
00:05:30,530 --> 00:05:32,530
But I have promises to keep,

102
00:05:32,530 --> 00:05:35,530
And miles to go before I sleep,

103
00:05:35,530 --> 00:05:40,530
And miles to go before I sleep.

104
00:05:40,530 --> 00:05:48,530
You two belong on stage! That was wonderful!

105
00:05:48,530 --> 00:05:49,530
Grandpa!

106
00:05:49,530 --> 00:05:52,530
Dad ... Robbie. When did you come?

107
00:05:52,530 --> 00:05:54,530
We've been listening to you both.

108
00:05:54,530 --> 00:05:56,530
These are lucky kids.

109
00:05:56,530 --> 00:05:59,530
Do you enjoy reading together?

110
00:05:59,530 --> 00:06:04,530
Well, we may read together aloud at home.

111
00:06:04,530 --> 00:06:06,530
You were right, Robbie.

112
00:06:06,530 --> 00:06:09,530
I know.
